  <h2>First, why these bills are big</h2>
  <p>Mitigation is mostly equipment and visits. A typical water job bills for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Emergency service and water extraction.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Equipment per day:</strong> air movers, dehumidifiers, sometimes air scrubbers. Several machines for several days adds up fast.</li>
    <li><strong>Daily monitoring:</strong> a technician's visit to take moisture readings and adjust equipment.</li>
    <li><strong>Removal and disposal</strong> of materials that can't be dried: soaked carpet pad, wet drywall, baseboards.</li>
    <li><strong>Antimicrobial application</strong> and <strong>containment</strong> where needed, especially with gray or black water.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>None of that is suspicious on its own. What you want to check is whether the <strong>quantities</strong> match what the drying actually needed.</p>

  <h2>Ask for the full file</h2>
  <p>Before arguing about the number, ask the company in writing for:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>The <strong>itemized invoice</strong>, with quantities and unit prices.</li>
    <li>The <strong>drying logs:</strong> daily moisture readings at each monitored point, with the drying goal.</li>
    <li>The <strong>equipment log:</strong> which machines were in which room on which days.</li>
    <li><strong>Photos</strong> before, during and after, especially of anything removed.</li>
    <li>The <strong>signed work authorization</strong> and any change orders.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Professional water damage restoration under the <strong>IICRC S500</strong> standard is measured and recorded. A company that did the work properly should be able to produce these quickly.</p>

  <h2>The charges most often questioned</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Line item</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Equipment days</strong></td><td>Did equipment stay after the logs show materials reached the drying goal?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Number of machines</strong></td><td>Does the count make sense for the affected area? Ask how it was calculated.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Demolition and tear-out</strong></td><td>Do photos show the material was wet or contaminated? Was it actually removed?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Water category</strong></td><td>Was clean water billed as gray or black? Category drives protective equipment, antimicrobials and what has to go. See <a href="/guides/water-damage-categories/">the three categories</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Monitoring visits</strong></td><td>Do the dates match the visits you remember?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>After-hours or emergency charges</strong></td><td>Were they disclosed when you called?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>When insurance pays less than the bill</h2>
  <p>This is the most common version of the problem. The insurer writes its own estimate, often with the same estimating software, and it comes out lower. Usually the difference sits in a few lines.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Get both documents side by side:</strong> the company's invoice and the insurer's estimate.</li>
    <li><strong>Mark each line</strong> as matching, different quantity, or missing.</li>
    <li><strong>Send the company's documentation to the adjuster</strong> for the differences, especially drying logs and photos, and ask for a written explanation of anything still denied.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask the company to talk to the adjuster directly.</strong> Many disputes resolve once both sides look at the same logs.</li>
    <li><strong>Check whether some items simply aren't covered</strong>, such as a gradual leak, or mold above a policy limit. See <a href="/guides/does-homeowners-insurance-cover-water-damage/">what insurance covers</a>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>If you're still stuck</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Write a dispute letter</strong> to the company listing each line you question and why. Offer to pay the undisputed amount.</li>
    <li><strong>Call your state insurance department's consumer line</strong> if the problem is how the insurer handled the claim.</li>
    <li><strong>Check your policy for an appraisal clause</strong>, used for disagreements about the amount of loss, and ask whether your state runs a mediation program.</li>
    <li><strong>For large amounts,</strong> a lawyer or a licensed public adjuster can advise on your options.</li>
    <li><strong>Small claims court</strong> is an option for modest amounts in many states.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Throughout, <strong>stay in writing and keep responding</strong>. Unpaid contractors can often file a lien on the property; a documented, good-faith dispute puts you in a far better position than silence.</p>

  <h2>Preventing it next time</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read the <a href="/guides/restoration-work-authorization-assignment-of-benefits/">work authorization</a> before signing, especially how the price is set.</li>
    <li>Ask on day one for daily moisture readings to be shared with you.</li>
    <li>Ask on day three, "What's left before we reach the drying goal?"</li>
  </ul>

